 weights\",\n412\t        \"rationale\": \"Best configuration adds gated domain-classifier importance weights (test mass at +inf, effective-sample-size margins); shift gap fell from 0.061 to 0.020 in search runs.\"\n413\t      },\n414\t      {\n415\t        \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n416\t        \"query\": \"density ratio estimation probabilistic classifier importance weighting covariate shift\",\n417\t        \"rationale\": \"Weights come from a cross-fitted ridge-logistic domain classifier with a held-out shift-detection gate, an implied density-ratio-by-classification literature.\"\n418\t      },\n419\t      {\n420\t        \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n421\t        \"query\": \"conformal prediction conditional coverage impossibility group-conditional guarantees\",\n422\t        \"rationale\": \"Worst-slice coverage gap (class/size/difficulty strata) stays 0.04-0.07 despite exact marginal validity (~0.001-0.0003 gap); must cite limits of distribution-free conditional coverage and group-conditional methods.\"\n423\t      },\n424\t      {\n425\t        \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n426\t        \"query\": \"conformal prediction conditional coverage via quantile regression of nonconformity scores\",\n427\t        \"rationale\": \"Third variant used group-indicator pinball quantile-regression thresholds with out-of-fold offset and exact fallback; it gave identical scores to the second variant (fallback/no gain), a result needing this literature.\"\n428\t      },\n429\t      {\n430\t        \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n431\t        \"query\": \"size-stratified coverage metric evaluating conformal classifiers adaptiveness\",\n432\t        \"rationale\": \"Reliability protocol is multi-axis (marginal gap, worst-slice gap, set-size ratio, shift gap, negative controls); need prior evaluation metrics for set-valued classifiers.\"\n433\t      },\n434\t      {\n435\t        \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n436\t        \"query\": \"compositional data analysis simplex log-ratio transformation Aitchison geometry\",\n437\t        \"rationale\": \"Outputs are softmax simplex vectors (Aitchison framing); motivates simplex-valued outputs and what is and is not exploited by scalar-score conformal methods.\"\n438\t      }\n439\t    ],\n440\t    \"result_driven_themes\": [\n441\t      \"Exact marginal validity coexists with residual worst-slice coverage gap\",\n442\t      \"Importance weighting reduces shift coverage gap but increases set size\",\n443\t      \"Quantile-regression conditional thresholds gave no measurable gain over weighted Mondrian in search\",\n444\t      \"Beats best baseline on final evaluation with CI excluding zero\"\n445\t    ],\n446\t    \"status\": \"ready\"\n447\t  },\n448\t  \"reference_query_plans\": [\n449\t    {\n450\t      \"architectural_shifts\": [\n451\t        \"Plain LAC -> Mondrian grouping -> gated importance-weighted Mondrian -> gated quantile-regression thresholds (no gain over previous variant)\"\n452\t      ],\n453\t      \"benchmarks\": [\n454\t        \"Evaluator-generated synthetic softmax families (K in {10,50,100}: exchangeable, temperature-miscalibrated, long-tailed prior, heteroscedastic, covariate/label shift)\",\n455\t        \"No real dataset (e.g. CIFAR-10) was used in the run\"\n456\t      ],\n457\t      \"central_topic\": \"Evaluating the reliability (marginal, conditional, shift-robust coverage versus set size) of split conformal prediction-set methods applied to compositional softmax outputs on synthetic multi-family benchmarks.\",\n458\t      \"comparators\": [\n459\t        \"LAC\",\n460\t        \"APS\",\n461\t        \"RAPS\",\n462\t        \"classwise conformal\",\n463\t        \"uncalibrated top-mass baseline\",\n464\t        \"trivial baselines\",\n465\t        \"negative controls (permuted calibration labels, shuffled test labels, constant uniform input)\"\n466\t      ],\n467\t      \"core_methods\": [\n468\t        \"Split conformal prediction with LAC score\",\n469\t        \"Mondrian (class-conditional / confidence-bin) conformal calibration\",\n470\t        \"Gated domain-classifier weighted conformal under covariate/label shift\",\n471\t        \"Group-indicator quantile-regression conformal thresholds with fallback\"\n472\t      ],\n473\t      \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n474\t        \"generic 'benchmarking' or 'optimization' phrasing\",\n475\t        \"campaign/search-history or milestone-shaped queries\",\n476\t        \"CIFAR-10 / real-image dataset queries as main topic (not used in run)\",\n477\t        \"regression-only conformal intervals unrelated to set-valued classification\",\n478\t        \"the withheld prior paper on this task, its title/authors/results or any page citing it\"\n479\t      ],\n480\t      \"queries\": [\n481\t        {\n482\t          \"category\": \"core_method\",\n483\t          \"query\": \"least ambiguous set-valued classifiers conformal LAC softmax score\",\n484\t          \"rationale\": \"LAC (1 - softmax of true class) is the base nonconformity score in all three variants; need its original set-valued-classifier source.\"\n485\t        },\n486\t        {\n487\t          \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n488\t          \"query\": \"adaptive prediction sets APS RAPS conformal classification regularization\",\n489\t          \"rationale\": \"Evaluator runs APS and RAPS as baselines on identical synthetic softmax data; final score margin over best baseline (+0.039, CI [0.027,0.050]) is the headline comparison.\"\n490\t        },\n491\t        {\n492\t          \"category\": \"core_method\",\n493\t          \"query\": \"Mondrian conformal prediction class-conditional label-conditional validity\",\n494\t          \"rationale\": \"Chosen configurations are Mondrian (class-conditional for K<=10, confidence-bin for K>10); classwise baseline also in evaluator.\"\n495\t        },\n496\t        {\n497\t          \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n498\t          \"query\": \"clustered conformal prediction many classes class-conditional coverage\",\n499\t          \"rationale\": \"K in {10,50,100} with limited per-class calibration data; confidence-bin grouping for K>10 relates to clustering/grouping remedies for class-conditional coverage.\"\n500\t        },\n501\t        {\n502\t          \"category\": \"core_method\",\n503\t          \"query\": \"weighted conformal prediction covariate shift label shift importance weights\",\n504\t          \"rationale\": \"Best configuration adds gated domain-classifier importance weights (test mass at +inf, effective-sample-size margins); shift gap fell from 0.061 to 0.020 in search runs.\"\n505\t        },\n506\t        {\n507\t          \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n508\t          \"query\": \"density ratio estimation probabilistic classifier importance weighting covariate shift\",\n509\t          \"rationale\": \"Weights come from a cross-fitted ridge-logistic domain classifier with a held-out shift-detection gate, an implied density-ratio-by-classification literature.\"\n510\t        },\n511\t        {\n512\t          \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n513\t          \"query\": \"conformal prediction conditional coverage impossibility group-conditional guarantees\",\n514\t          \"rationale\": \"Worst-slice coverage gap (class/size/difficulty strata) stays 0.04-0.07 despite exact marginal validity (~0.001-0.0003 gap); must cite limits of distribution-free conditional coverage and group-conditional methods.\"\n515\t        },\n516\t        {\n517\t          \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n518\t          \"query\": \"conformal prediction conditional coverage via quantile regression of nonconformity scores\",\n519\t          \"rationale\": \"Third variant used group-indicator pinball quantile-regression thresholds with out-of-fold offset and exact fallback; it gave identical scores to the second variant (fallback/no gain), a result needing this literature.\"\n520\t        },\n521\t        {\n522\t          \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n523\t          \"query\": \"size-stratified coverage metric evaluating conformal classifiers adaptiveness\",\n524\t          \"rationale\": \"Reliability protocol is multi-axis (marginal gap, worst-slice gap, set-size ratio, shift gap, negative controls); need prior evaluation metrics for set-valued classifiers.\"\n525\t        },\n526\t        {\n527\t          \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n528\t          \"query\": \"compositional data analysis simplex log-ratio transformation Aitchison geometry\",\n529\t          \"rationale\": \"Outputs are softmax simplex vectors (Aitchison framing); motivates simplex-valued outputs and what is and is not exploited by scalar-score conformal methods.\"\n530\t        }\n531\t      ],\n532\t      \"result_driven_themes\": [\n533\t        \"Exact marginal validity coexists with residual worst-slice coverage gap\",\n534\t        \"Importance weighting reduces shift coverage gap but increases set size\",\n535\t        \"Quantile-regression conditional thresholds gave no measurable gain over weighted Mondrian in search\",\n536\t        \"Beats best baseline on final evaluation with CI excluding zero\"\n537\t      ],\n538\t      \"status\": \"ready\"\n539\t    },\n540\t    {\n541\t      \"architectural_shifts\": [\n542\t        \"Plain LAC -> Mondrian grouping -> gated importance-weighted Mondrian -> gated quantile-regression thresholds (no gain over previous variant)\"\n543\t      ],\n544\t      \"benchmarks\": [\n545\t        \"Evaluator-generated synthetic softmax families (K in {10,50,100}: exchangeable, temperature-miscalibrated, long-tailed prior, heteroscedastic, covariate/label shift)\",\n546\t        \"No real dataset (e.g. CIFAR-10) was used in the run\"\n547\t      ],\n548\t      \"central_topic\": \"Evaluating the reliability (marginal, conditional, shift-robust coverage versus set size) of split conformal prediction-set methods applied to compositional softmax outputs on synthetic multi-family benchmarks.\",\n549\t      \"comparators\": [\n550\t        \"LAC\",\n551\t        \"APS\",\n552\t        \"RAPS\",\n553\t        \"classwise conformal\",\n554\t        \"uncalibrated top-mass baseline\",\n555\t        \"trivial baselines\",\n556\t        \"negative controls (permuted calibration labels, shuffled test labels, constant uniform input)\"\n557\t      ],\n558\t      \"core_methods\": [\n559\t        \"Split conformal prediction with LAC score\",\n560\t        \"Mondrian (class-conditional / confidence-bin) conformal calibration\",\n561\t        \"Gated domain-classifier weighted conformal under covariate/label shift\",\n562\t        \"Group-indicator quantile-regression conformal thresholds with fallback\"\n563\t      ],\n564\t      \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n565\t        \"generic 'benchmarking' or 'optimization' phrasing\",\n566\t        \"campaign/searc\n567\t\n568\t[truncated after 14000 chars]\n569\t```\n570\t\n571\t## manuscript source excerpt\n572\t\n573\t```tex\n574\t\\documentclass{article}\n575\t\n576\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n577\t\\usepackage{xfp}\n578\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n579\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n580\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{\n581\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n582\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}\n583\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}\n584\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n585\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}\n586\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}\n587\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}\n588\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}\n589\t}\n590\t\n591\t \n592\t\\usepackage{paper,times}\n593\t\n594\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n595\t\\usepackage{url}\n596\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n597\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n598\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n599\t\n600\t\\newcommand{\\score}{\\mathrm{Score}}\n601\t\n602\t\\title{Gated Weighted-Mondrian Conformal Sets for Softmax Outputs: A Multi-Axis Reliability Evaluation on Synthetic Simplex Families}\n603\t\\author{Anonymous authors}\n604\t\n605\t\\begin{document}\n606\t\\maketitle\n607\t\n608\t\\begin{abstract}\n609\tSoftmax outputs are compositions: non-negative vectors summing to one. Conformal prediction turns them into label sets with a finite-sample marginal coverage guarantee, but marginal coverage says little about reliability for particular classes, confidence strata, or shifted data. We study a split-conformal procedure that combines Mondrian (class- or confidence-bin-conditional) calibration of the least-ambiguous-set score with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step that reduces exactly to the unweighted procedure when no shift is detected. We evaluate it under a fixed, evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families: a composite score combining one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio, plus negative controls. On sealed final data (five seeded runs, no failed episodes, all controls passed) the procedure scores 0.8416 against 0.8029 for the best of four standard split-conformal baselines; the paired margin is 0.0387 (bootstrap 95\\% interval 0.0274--0.0499). Residual under-coverage remains on shifted cases (0.0536) and worst slices (0.0675). The result is conditional on the declared synthetic families; baseline identities, per-case results and real-data behaviour are unavailable.\n610\t\\end{abstract}\n611\t\n612\t\\section{Introduction}\n613\tMany predictors return a composition: a vector of non-negative entries summing to one, such as a classifier's softmax probabilities. Users of such outputs need to know when the associated uncertainty statement can be trusted. Conformal prediction converts a score function and a held-out calibration set into prediction sets whose marginal coverage is at least $1-\\alpha$ under exchangeability, in finite samples and without distributional assumptions. In practice, however, three questions that matter to a user are not answered by the marginal guarantee: does coverage hold within classes or confidence strata, does it survive a change in the input distribution, and is it achieved with sets that are small enough to be informative? Our motivation is that a method tuned for one question may do worse on another, so reporting only average coverage and set size can hide such differences; conditional-coverage and efficiency work \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Kiyani2024LengthOI} makes the individual questions concrete.\n614\t\n615\tThis paper takes the evaluation protocol as its first-class object. We adopt a single evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families that scores a set-valued predictor on three axes at once (one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio) and that adds negative controls whose expected behaviour is fixed before scoring. Within this protocol we ask:\n616\t\\emph{does a Mondrian least-ambiguous-set conformal procedure with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step achieve a better composite reliability score than standard split-conformal baselines, and where do its guarantees visibly stop holding?}\n617\t\n618\tThe answer we can support is conditional. On sealed final data the studied procedure obtains a higher composite score than the best of four evaluator-implemented baselines, with a paired margin whose bootstrap interval excludes zero; it also shows a small one-sided marginal under-coverage gap (0.0017) on exchangeable cases. At the same time, the sealed metrics show non-trivial residual under-coverage on shifted cases and on worst slices, and the retained artifacts do not allow us to say which baseline was best, which cases drive the residual gaps, or whether the result extends beyond the declared families.\n619\t\n620\t\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n621\t(i) We write down the three-axis protocol in reader-checkable form, including the loss, the slice definitions, the negative controls and the validity conditions, and derive simple properties relevant to interpreting scores (for example, that the full prediction set scores exactly 0.75 and that the one-sided gap metrics do not penalise over-coverage).\n622\t(ii) We specify one concrete procedure, composed from known pieces (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, a held-out gate), and state which of its properties are exact and which are only approximate when the weights are estimated.\n623\t(iii) We report a sealed final comparison with uncertainty and decompose the residual loss into its marginal, slice and size components.\n624\t(iv) We delimit scope: the evidence concerns synthetic families, one sealed evaluation, and one aggregated baseline comparison. We make no claim of a new theorem or of a compositional-geometry method; the procedure operates on summary features of the probability vector.\n625\t\n626\t\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n627\t\\centering\n628\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n629\t\\caption{Evaluation protocol at a glance. Simplex-valued softmax outputs from evaluator-generated synthetic families are split into calibration and test parts. The studied procedure (Mondrian LAC with gated domain-classifier weighting) and four evaluator baselines (LAC, APS, RAPS, class-wise LAC) produce label sets, which are scored on three axes (marginal under-coverage with weight 0.5, worst-slice under-coverage 0.25, set-size ratio 0.25) and checked by negative controls. Scope is limited to the declared synthetic families.}\n630\t\\label{fig:overview}\n631\t\\end{figure}\n632\t\n633\t\\section{Related Work}\\label{sec:related}\n634\t\\paragraph{Foundations and classification scores.}\n635\tSplit conformal prediction uses a held-out calibration set and retains finite-sample marginal coverage under exchangeability \\citep{Vovk2005Algorithmic,Lei2018Distribution}; Mondrian conformal prediction applies the same rank argument within partition cells \\citep{Vovk2005Algorithmic}. For classifiers, the least-ambiguous-set construction thresholds the softmax mass of each label \\citep{Sadinle2019Least}, adaptive prediction sets accumulate sorted probability mass with randomisation \\citep{Romano2020Classification}, and a regularised variant penalises large ranks to shrink sets on many-class problems \\citep{Angelopoulos2020Uncertainty}. These are our comparators (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n636\t\n637\t\\paragraph{Conditional validity.}\n638\tExact conditional coverage is impossible in general without further structure, which has motivated relaxations that target class-, group- or feature-conditional guarantees \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP}. Conditional conformal prediction has been proposed for bias reduction \\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses class-conditional coverage with many classes \\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP}, conformal prediction for long-tailed classification \\citep{Ding2025ConformalPF}, and class-wise coverage via augmented label rank calibration \\citep{Shi2024ConformalPF}. Group-conditional thresholds have also been learned by quantile regression \\citep{Melki2023GroupConditionalCP}, trust scores have been used to improve conditional coverage \\citep{Kaur2025ConformalPS}, and coverage conditional on a selection event has been studied \\citep{Jin2024ConfidenceOT}. Our procedure uses the simplest member of this family, Mondrian groups defined by label or by confidence bin, and we examine a quantile-regression variant only as a development-time comparison.\n639\t\n640\t\\paragraph{Efficiency and the classifier.}\n641\tSet size is a second axis: length-optimal conformal prediction \\citep{Kiyani2024LengthOI}, classifier adaptation for efficient sets \\citep{Liu2024CAdapterAD}, the interaction between temperature scaling and conformal sets \\citep{Dabah2024OnTS}, and the quality of foundation-model features as conformal predictors \\citep{Fillioux2024AreFM} all bear on how small a valid set can be. Conformal methods have also been combined with Monte Carlo prediction \\citep{Bethell2023RobustUQ} and interpretable networks \\citep{Huang2024CONFINECP}.\n642\t\n643\t\\paragraph{Other output types and settings.}\n644\tConformal methods have been developed for time series \\citep{Xu2022ConformalPS}, ordinal labels \\citep{Chakraborty2024DistributionfreeCP}, heteroskedastic regression with conditional validity \\citep{Dewolf2023ConditionalVO}, multivariate residuals \\citep{Braun2025MultivariateSR}, epistemic-uncertainty settings \\citep{Javanmardi2025OptimalCP} and connections to scenario optimisation \\citep{O'Sullivan2025BridgingCP}. Applied treatments of conformal classifiers appear in clinical imaging and histopathology \\citep{Cina2026QuantifyingCC,Gelvez2025ConformalPF}. We describe these works only by title and venue.\n645\t\n646\t\\paragraph{Distribution shift.}\n647\tUnder covariate shift, exchangeability fails. If the density ratio is known, reweighting the calibration scores by it and giving the test point its own weight restores marginal coverage \\citep{Tibshirani2019Conformal}; estimating the ratio with a calibration-versus-test classifier makes the guarantee approximate. Our gated step follows this recipe with an explicit unweighted fallback.\n648\t\n649\t\\paragraph{Positioning.}\n650\tOur contribution is neither a new conformal score nor a new guarantee. It is a combined reading of one evaluation protocol and one composition of established components (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, and a held-out gate), with sealed evidence and an explicit account of where it fails. Compositional-data analysis in the sense of log-ratio geometry \\citep{Aitchison1982Statistical} motivates the framing (outputs live on the simplex), but the procedure studied here does not use log-ratio coordinates for its prediction sets.\n651\t\n652\t\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n653\t\\subsection{Problem setup and evaluation target}\\label{sec:setup}\n654\tLet $p(x)\\in\\Delta^{K-1}$ be the softmax output of a fixed $K$-class predictor, with $y\\in\\{1,\\dots,K\\}$. A conformal procedure receives $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}$ labelled calibration pairs $(p_i,y_i)$, the level $\\alpha$, and $n_{\\mathrm{test}}$ unlabelled test vectors, and returns a set $\\mathcal C(x)\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,K\\}$ for each test vector. The evaluator owns the data: logits are $z=\\epsilon+s\\,d\\,e_y+\\beta\\log\\pi_{\\mathrm{cal}}$ with $\\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I_K)$, signal strength $s$, a log-normal heteroscedastic multiplier $d$ on the true-class signal, a prior tilt $\\beta$, and temperature scaling before the softmax; the class prior is balanced or long-tailed. Test data may differ from calibration data through the class prior and a signal-strength multiplier (``shifted'' cases); otherwise a case is exchangeable. The declared range is $2\\le K\\le 100$, $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}\\le 2000$, $n_{\\mathrm{test}}\\le 4000$ in the search-time evaluator source; the sealed final case manifest (families, $K$, $\\alpha$, sample sizes) is not retained and is therefore unknown to us.\n655\t\n656\t\n657\t\\subsection{Composite reliability score}\n658\tFor one episode with set indicator $\\mathcal C(x_j)$ on $n$ test points, let $\\mathrm{cov}$ be empirical coverage, $\\rho=\\frac1{nK}\\sum_j|\\mathcal C(x_j)|$ the set-size ratio, and $g_{\\mathrm{m}}=\\max(0,1-\\alpha-\\mathrm{cov})$ the one-sided marginal gap. The worst-slice gap $g_{\\mathrm{s}}$ is the largest one-sided coverage shortfall over slices with at least 30 test points: classes when $K\\le10$, and strata of set size and of maximum probability (quartiles) when $K>10$. The episode loss and score are\n659\t\\begin{equation}\n660\t\\ell=0.5\\,\\mathrm{clip}_{[0,1]}(g_{\\mathrm m}/\\alpha)+0.25\\,\\mathrm{clip}_{[0,1]}(g_{\\mathrm s})+0.25\\,\\mathrm{clip}_{[0,1]}(\\rho),\\qquad \\score=1-\\overline{\\ell},\n661\t\\label{eq:loss}\n662\t\\end{equation}\n663\twhere $\\overline\\ell$ averages over seeds within a case and with equal weight over cases. Two properties follow directly from Eq.~\\eqref{eq:loss}. First, the full set has $g_{\\mathrm m}=g_{\\mathrm s}=0$ and $\\rho=1$, so it scores exactly $0.75$ on every case; any useful procedure must beat this by shrinking sets without opening coverage gaps. Second, the gaps are one-sided: over-coverage is penalised only through $\\rho$, so a small gap does not show that coverage is tight. Shift-case coverage enters $\\score$ through the same terms, but the shift gap is reported as a descriptive metric and is not a validity condition.\n664\t\n665\t\n666\t\\subsection{Baselines, negative controls and validity conditions}\n667\tThe evaluator scores four standard split-conformal baselines on identical data with the same loss, drawn from the score families of Section~\\ref{sec:related}: LAC \\citep{Sadinle2019Least} (threshold on $1-p_y$), APS \\citep{Romano2020Classification} (randomised cumulative-mass score), RAPS \\citep{Angelopoulos2020Uncertainty} (APS with a rank penalty, $\\lambda=0.01$, regularisation start $\\min(5,\\max(1,\\lfloor K/5\\rfloor))$) and class-wise LAC (LAC calibrated per label). Exact implementations are those of the evaluator source, not re-derived from the cited papers. Uncalibrated top-mass, full-set and argmax-only sets are implemented as trivial references. The reported baseline score is that of the best of the four baselines under the aggregated score; the margin is $\\score_{\\mathrm{proc}}-\\score_{\\mathrm{best}}$, and its 95\\% interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile of 2000 bootstrap resamples of the per-seed paired differences within each case, averaged with equal weight over cases. The interval therefore reflects seed-to-seed variation inside the evaluated cases and not variation across case designs.\n668\t\n669\tIn the search-plane evaluator source, three negative controls are defined, each run on one case and seed: (C1) permuted calibration labels, where sets must inflate (size ratio at least $1.5\\times$ the matched case or at least $0.9$); (C2) shuffled test labels, where coverage must not exceed chance by more than a $3\\sigma$ tolerance and must be well below matched coverage or sets near full; (C3) uniform probability inputs, where coverage must be at least the target minus a tolerance and mean set size at least $(1-\\alpha)K-1$; this check is one-sided, so over-coverage is not penalised by it. The number of final-plane controls is not retained; only a controls-passed fraction of 1.0 is. A run is valid only if no episode fails, all controls pass, calibration and test data are content-hash disjoint (and, for the final plane, disjoint from the search data), and exchangeable cases do not under-cover by more than a $3\\sigma$ finite-sample tolerance.\n670\t\n671\t\n672\t\\subsection{Statistical background and what it predicts}\n673\t\\paragraph{Marginal and Mondrian validity.}\n674\tWith exchangeable calibration and test points and continuous scores, split conformal with threshold equal to the $k$-th smallest of $n$ calibration scores, $k=\\lceil (n+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, covers with probability $k/(n+1)\\in[1-\\alpha,\\,1-\\alpha+1/(n+1))$, and coverage conditional on the calibration set follows a $\\mathrm{Beta}(k,n+1-k)$ law \\citep{Vovk2005Algorithmic,Lei2018Distribution}. Applying the same rank argument within each cell of a partition $G(x,y)$ yields cell-conditional coverage (Mondrian calibration, \\citealt{Vovk2005Algorithmic}); with $G$ equal to the label this is class-conditional coverage, and with $G$ a function of $x$ alone it is conditional on the stratum. These are textbook statements \\citep{Vovk2005Algorithmic} and we do not claim them as new.\n675\t\n676\t\\paragraph{Proposition (Mondrian coverage; textbook).}\n677\tLet $G$ be a partition cell function of $(x,y)$ and let the calibration scores of cell $g$ be $s_1,\\dots,s_{n_g}$, exchangeable with the test score $s_{\\mathrm{new}}$ conditional on the test point lying in cell $g$, with no ties. Take $q_g$ equal to the $k_g$-th smallest calibration score, $k_g=\\lceil(n_g+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$. Then $\\Pr(s_{\\mathrm{new}}\\le q_g\\mid G=g)\\ge1-\\alpha$.\n678\t\\emph{Proof sketch.} By exchangeability the rank of $s_{\\mathrm{new}}$ among the $n_g+1$ scores is uniform on $\\{1,\\dots,n_g+1\\}$. The event $s_{\\mathrm{new}}\\le q_g$ holds exactly when this rank is at most $k_g$, which has probability $k_g/(n_g+1)\\ge1-\\alpha$; ties only increase the probability. $\\square$\n679\tFor $G=y$ the cell is observable only at calibration time, and the set $\\{k:1-p_k(x)\\le q_k\\}$ covers the true label $y$ with class-conditional probability at least $1-\\alpha$ because the event is $\\{s(x,y)\\le q_y\\}$. For $G$ a function of $x$ alone the cell is known at prediction time. The guarantee is conditional on the cell and does not extend to other partitions: when $K>10$ the evaluator's slices (strata of set size and of maximum probability in quartiles) differ from the Mondrian confidence bins used here. The implemented thresholds also depart from the proposition: lowering $\\alpha_g$ by a margin can only raise $q_g$, but shrinking $q_g$ toward the pooled threshold can lower it, so the implemented procedure has no exact cell-conditional guarantee, only the exact quantile at level $\\alpha_g$ does. The data-dependent gate below is \\emph{not} covered by this argument: it uses unlabelled calibration and test features, it treats the two groups asymmetrically, and we have not derived a guarantee for the draws on which it activates under exchangeability. We therefore use the proposition only as background for the fallback branch.\n680\t\n681\t\\paragraph{Weighted calibration under covariate shift.}\n682\tIf test inputs follow density ratio $w(x)$ with respect to calibration inputs and $w$ is known, placing the test point's weight at $+\\infty$ in the weighted empirical distribution of calibration scores restores marginal coverage \\citep{Tibshirani2019Conformal}; with an estimated $w$ the guarantee is only approximate. Our procedure estimates $w$ with a classifier on summary features, so we treat the weighted branch as a heuristic correction whose benefit has to be measured, not as a guaranteed one.\n683\t\n684\t\\paragraph{Why a one-sided loss favours a small margin.}\n685\tUnder the normal approximation to the Beta law, calibration-induced coverage is $C\\approx\\mathcal N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ with $\\sigma^2\\approx\\alpha(1-\\alpha)/(n+2)$ and $\\mu\\ge1-\\alpha$. The expected one-sided shortfall is $\\sigma\\,f(\\delta)$ with $f(\\delta)=\\varphi(\\delta)-\\delta(1-\\Phi(\\delta))$ and $\\delta=(\\mu-(1-\\alpha))/\\sigma$. An exact-level quantile has $\\delta\\approx0$ and $f(0)=0.399$, whereas shifting the level by $0.7\\sigma$ gives $f(0.7)=0.143$, about 36\\% of the unmargined value, at the price of larger sets. This is the motivation for the variance-aware margins used below (multipliers 0.7 pooled, 0.6 per group). It is a heuristic derivation about calibration noise only; we have no unmargined ablation, so it is not checked experimentally here.\n686\t\n687\t\n688\t\\subsection{Design rationale and hypotheses}\n689\tThe procedure is built around three expectations, each tied to one component. \\textbf{H1}: grouping calibration by label (small $K$) or confidence bin (large $K$) should shrink worst-slice under-coverage relative to pooled calibration, because it makes the coverage guarantee hold within the evaluator's slices when those coincide with the groups. \\textbf{H2}: a gated, cross-fitted density-ratio correction should reduce marginal under-coverage on shifted cases, while a gate that stays closed leaves exchangeable behaviour and the negative controls untouched. \\textbf{H3}: group-indicator quantile-regression thresholds could further reduce worst-slice gaps when the groups are overlapping. The retained evidence addresses these only partially: H1 is not isolated (no pooled-versus-Mondrian pair of otherwise identical configurations was retained, and baseline identities are unknown); H2 is addressed by the development comparison V1 versus V2 and by the sealed shift-case gap; H3 is untestable from V3 because V3 coincides with V2. The aim of the sealed evaluation is thus the narrower statement of Section~\\ref{sec:results} about the composite score, not a verification of each hypothesis.\n690\t\n691\t\\subsection{Studied procedure}\n692\t\\paragraph{Mondrian LAC.}\n693\tThe score of label $k$ is $1-p_k(x)$ (LAC). For $K\\le10$ the Mondrian groups are the labels, because the evaluator's slices are classes; for $K>10$ they are confidence bins of the maximum probability (at most 10 equal-mass bins, at least 80 calibration rows each), a function of $x$ alone. Each group's threshold is the finite-sample conformal quantile at level $\\alpha_g=\\alpha-z\\sqrt{\\alpha(1-\\alpha)(1/n_g+k_g/M)}$ with $z=0.6$ per group and $0.7$ for the pooled threshold, where $M$ is the number of test rows and $k_g$ the number of groups; group thresholds are shrunk toward the pooled threshold with weight $n_g/(n_g+20)$, and groups too small for a finite quantile use the pooled value. The set is $\\{k: 1-p_k(x)\\le q_{g}\\}$.\n694\t\n695\t\n696\t\\paragraph{Gated cross-fitted weighting.}\n697\tEach probability vector is mapped to five summary features (logit of the top probability, top-two log ratio, normalised entropy, mean top-five log probability minus the top log probability, log normalised Gini impurity), standardised with calibration moments and clipped to $\\pm8$. As test chunks arrive, a 2-fold cross-fitted ridge-penalised logistic classifier (penalty 1, class-balanced weights) separates calibration from all test rows seen so far, and the held-out likelihood-ratio statistic $\\mathrm{LR}=2(\\ell_{\\mathrm{full}}-\\ell_{\\mathrm{null}})$ against an intercept-only model gives a positive-part shrinkage factor $\\beta=\\max(0,1-d/\\mathrm{LR})$ with $d=5$. If fewer than 40 test rows have been seen, or $\\beta\\le10^{-3}$, or any quantity is non-finite, the unweighted Mondrian thresholds are used verbatim. Otherwise weights $w=\\exp(\\mathrm{clip}(\\beta z,\\pm\\log 8))$ are built from the centred held-out log-odds $z$, and each test row receives weighted per-group thresholds with its own weight at $+\\infty$ and effective-sample-size margins ($n_{\\mathrm{eff}}=(\\sum w)^2/\\sum w^2$). Under exchangeability the gate is intended to be inactive because the held-out likelihood ratio is typically non-positive; it is not guaranteed to be inactive on every draw, and its activation frequency was not logged.\n698\t\n699\t\n700\t\\subsection{Compared configurations}\n701\t\\emph{Baselines} are the four evaluator-implemented split-conformal procedures above; only the best baseline score and the paired margin are retained. \\emph{The studied procedure} is Mondrian LAC with gated weighting; it is the configuration we take to have been frozen by the selection step and evaluated on sealed data (see the caveat on identity below). Two nested development configurations bracket it: \\textbf{V1} is Mondrian LAC without weighting, \\textbf{V2} (the studied procedure) adds the gated weighting, and \\textbf{V3} adds, on top of V2, cross-fitted group-indicator quantile-regression thresholds that are activated only by a calibration-only cross-validation gate and otherwise fall back to the V2 path verbatim (also when $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}<300$ or on failure). V1--V3 were scored on development data and are a design comparison, not a significance-tested ablation.\n702\t\n703\t\n704\t\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\n705\t\\paragraph{Development data.} V1--V3 are scored with seeds 101--103 on the evaluator's development cases (per the declared reset sequence, three seeds on each of four case designs, plus the three controls); all three configurations produced valid runs and none was unscored. The development scores of V2 and V3 tie exactly. We attribute the sealed evaluation to V2 on the basis of its position in the development ranking (tied with V3); the selection manifest referenced by the evaluation record is not retained in the workspace, so this identity is not independently verified.\n706\t\\paragraph{Sealed final data.} The frozen procedure is evaluated on sealed cases with seeds 9001--9005, disjoint from the development data by content hash. Each seeded run's score is the mean over cases of $1-\\ell$; the reported final score is the equal-weight mean over seeds and cases. Development and final numbers use different cases and seeds and are never pooled.\n707\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} We report the five per-seed final scores, their sample standard deviation, and the evaluator's bootstrap interval for the paired margin. For the three development seeds we report paired per-seed differences without a significance test: with three pairs, the smallest attainable two-sided exact sign-flip $p$-value is $0.25$.\n708\t\n709\t\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n710\t\\subsection{Sealed final comparison}\n711\tThe studied procedure attains a sealed final score of 0.8416 against 0.8029 for the best baseline (Table~\\ref{tab:final}, Figure~\\ref{fig:final}). \n712\tThe paired margin is 0.0387 with bootstrap 95\\% interval 0.0274--0.0499, which excludes zero. \n713\tIn loss terms the mean loss falls from 0.1971 to 0.1584, a relative reduction of 19.6\\%. \n714\tThe five seeded runs score between 0.812 and 0.862 (mean 0.8416, standard deviation 0.019), so every seeded run is above the aggregate best-baseline score, although this compares individual seeds with an aggregate. \n715\tNo episode failed and all negative controls passed (fraction 1.0; count not retained), and the sealed qualification outcome is ``qualified'' with status ``completed''. \n716\t\n717\t\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n718\t\\centering\n719\t\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth]{figures/final_comparison.pdf}\n720\t\\caption{Sealed final comparison. (a) Paired score margin of the studied procedure over the best of four baselines with its 95\\% bootstrap interval (seeded runs within cases, equal weight over cases). (b) Per-seed final scores of the procedure; horizontal lines mark the procedure's aggregate score and the best-baseline aggregate score, so per-seed dots are compared with an aggregate.}\n721\t\\label{fig:final}\n722\t\\end{figure}\n723\t\n724\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n725\t\\centering\n726\t\\caption{Sealed final metrics retained by the evaluator. Gaps are one-sided shortfalls; ``shift'' is descriptive.}\n727\t\\label{tab:final}\n728\t\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n729\t\\toprule\n730\tMetric & Value \\\\\n731\t\\midrule\n732\tComposite score of studied procedure & 0.8416 \\\\\n733\tComposite score of best baseline (identity not retained) & 0.8029 \\\\\n734\tPaired margin (95\\% bootstrap interval) & 0.0387 (0.0274, 0.0499) \\\\\n735\tExchangeable-case marginal under-coverage gap & 0.0017 \\\\\n736\tWorst-slice under-coverage gap (all cases) & 0.0675 \\\\\n737\tShift-case marginal under-coverage gap & 0.0536 \\\\\n738\tMean set-size ratio & 0.209 \\\\\n739\tNegative controls passed (fraction; count not retained) & 1.0 \\\\\n740\tFailed episodes & 0 \\\\\n741\t\\bottomrule\n742\t\\end{tabular}\n743\t\n744\t\\end{table}\n745\t\n746\t\\subsection{Reliability profile and residual failures}\n747\tThe headline margin should be read together with the residual gaps. On exchangeable cases the mean marginal under-coverage gap is 0.0017, compatible with the finite-sample guarantee; because the gap is one-sided and averaged over a few seeded runs, this is not a test of the guarantee and does not show coverage is tight. The mean set-size ratio is 0.209. The shift-case marginal gap is 0.0536 and the worst-slice gap is 0.0675, so both residual failures are larger than the exchangeable gap by more than an order of magnitude. \n748\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3826cce8-73e2-4c91-9dfb-caa067835519","uuid":"b8c32e4f-4861-4ce2-a64e-d1a7088c05fd","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:29:16.231Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper_evidence_audit_packet.md","content":"# Agentic evidence audit packet\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T10:29:12.594769+00:00`\n- paper_dir: `work/results/r02/claude/20261111_085834/.paper_evidence_audit_workspaces-1000/agentic-evidence-audit-1000-20261111_094241-paper_evidence_audit_round_5-l9utkaee/workspace`\n\nUse this packet as the default entrypoint. Read raw artifacts only when this packet is insufficient.\nThe goal is narrow: check whether the paper's visible claims remain consistent with claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json, and the scientific-evidence trail.\n\n## Expected audit focus\n\n- visible claims in `paper.tex` that overstate, contradict, or drift from `claims_grounding.json`\n- claims grounded to the wrong source ids, or source ids that do not support the visible wording\n- mismatches between visible manuscript text and the resolved/anchored evidence trail\n- cases where the evidence trail exists but does not justify the paper's wording or numeric precision\n- contradictions between `paper.tex`, `claims_grounding.json`, `paper_data.json`, and `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- every visible claim in Supplementary Information, including tables and figures, under the same grounding standard as the main paper\n\n## Artifact checklist\n\n- `paper.tex` — `present`\n- `claims_grounding.json` — `present`\n- `paper_data.json` — `present`\n- `validation_report.json` — `present`\n- `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` — `present`\n- `references_provenance.json` — `present`\n\n## validation report summary\n\n```json\n{\n  \"available\": true,\n  \"evidence_validation\": {\n    \"anchored_references\": 42,\n    \"bundle_present\": true,\n    \"checked\": true,\n    \"dangling_references\": 0,\n    \"paper_token_count\": 59,\n    \"resolved_references\": 33,\n    \"total_references\": 75\n  },\n  \"ok\": false,\n  \"pdf_path\": null\n}\n```\n\n## scientific-evidence verification summary\n\n```json\n{\n  \"available\": true,\n  \"dangling_preview\": [],\n  \"summary\": {\n    \"anchored_references\": 42,\n    \"dangling_references\": 0,\n    \"manifests_checked\": 8,\n    \"resolved_references\": 33,\n    \"total_references\": 75\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## claims_grounding excerpt\n\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Sealed final composite score of the studied procedure is 0.8416.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.final_score\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Best of four baselines scores 0.8029 on sealed final data (identity not retained).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.best_baseline_score\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Paired margin 0.0387 with bootstrap 95% interval 0.0274-0.0499 (excludes zero).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.score_margin_vs_best_baseline\",\n        \"final.metric.score_margin_ci_lower\",\n        \"final.metric.score_margin_ci_upper\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Exchangeable-case marginal under-coverage gap is 0.0017.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.mean_marginal_coverage_gap_exchangeable\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Worst-slice gap 0.0675; shift-case marginal gap 0.0536; mean set-size ratio 0.209.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.worst_slice_coverage_gap\",\n        \"final.metric.shift_coverage_gap\",\n        \"final.metric.mean_set_size_ratio\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"No failed episodes and all negative controls passed (controls-passed fraction 1.0; number of final-plane controls not retained).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.n_failed_episodes\",\n        \"final.metric.controls_passed_fraction\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Sealed qualification outcome is qualified with status completed and correct true.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.qualification.outcome\",\n        \"final.qualification.status\",\n        \"final.qualification.correct\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Five per-seed final scores (seeds 9001-9005) range 0.812-0.862 with mean 0.8416 and standard deviation 0.019; per-seed durations 4-8 s (derived in derived/derived_stats.json final_per_seed).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n        \"final.metric.final_score\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Mean loss falls from 0.1971 to 0.1584, a relative reduction of 19.6% (derived: 1 - score; derived/derived_stats.json final_loss_decomposition).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.final_score\",\n        \"final.metric.best_baseline_score\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Loss decomposition: set-size term 0.0522, worst-slice term 0.0169, implied marginal term 0.0893 (mean clipped marginal gap over alpha 0.179), derived from retained case-mean metrics via the 0.5/0.25/0.25 loss.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.final_score\",\n        \"final.metric.mean_set_size_ratio\",\n        \"final.metric.worst_slice_coverage_gap\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Interval half-width about 0.011 and margin roughly twice the per-seed standard deviation 0.019 (derived).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.score_margin_ci_lower\",\n        \"final.metric.score_margin_ci_upper\",\n        \"final.metric.score_margin_vs_best_baseline\",\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Full set scores exactly 0.75 under the loss definition (analytic from loss weights in evaluator source source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate.py).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.qualification.status\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Development scores: V1 (Mondrian LAC) 0.8683, V2 (gated weighting) 0.9192, V3 (QR thresholds) 0.9192, identical per-seed scores for V2 and V3; 3 of 3 scored, none unscored.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"result.improvement_001\",\n        \"result.improvement_002\",\n        \"result.top_program_01\",\n        \"result.top_program_02\",\n        \"result.top_program_03\",\n        \"result.total_attempts\",\n        \"result.total_evaluated_programs\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Development secondary metrics (from per-variant evaluator metric files results/ideas/*/program_1/metrics.json): shift gap 0.0613 -> 0.0201, worst-slice gap 0.0497 -> 0.0376, set-size ratio 0.166 -> 0.181, exchangeable gap 0.00028 for V1 and V2; V3 equals V2.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"result.improvement_001\",\n        \"result.improvement_002\",\n        \"result.top_program_03\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Paired per-seed development differences V2-V1 are 0.064, 0.043, 0.045; with three pairs the smallest two-sided exact sign-flip p-value is 0.25 (derived).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"result.improvement_001\",\n        \"result.improvement_002\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Development score of the selected procedure (0.9192) exceeds its sealed final score (0.8416); development shift gap 0.0201 and size ratio 0.181 versus final 0.0536 and 0.209; cause not isolated.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"result.best_correct_score\",\n        \"final.metric.final_score\",\n        \"final.metric.shift_coverage_gap\",\n        \"final.metric.mean_set_size_ratio\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Final seeds 9001-9005 and development seeds 101-103; frozen procedure evaluated on sealed cases (single-run status and the frozen configuration's identity are not independently verified).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n        \"result.top_program_01\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Margin-theory constants f(0)=0.399, f(0.7)=0.143 (ratio 0.36) from normal-approximation derivation; heuristic, not checked experimentally.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.mean_marginal_coverage_gap_exchangeable\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [\n        \"Gibbs2023ConformalPW\",\n        \"Ding2023ClassConditionalCP\",\n        \"Shi2024ConformalPF\",\n        \"Melki2023GroupConditionalCP\",\n        \"Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP\",\n        \"Kaur2025ConformalPS\",\n        \"Jin2024ConfidenceOT\",\n        \"L\\u00f6fstr\\u00f6m2015BiasRT\",\n        \"Ding2025ConformalPF\"\n      ],\n      \"claim\": \"Related work: conditional and class-conditional conformal prediction, many/long-tailed classes, group-conditional QR calibration, Kandinsky, selection-conditional and trust-score variants (descriptions limited to titles and venues).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"ref.s2.206ac9b41d419be0da5acc198f574e5bfa3c6548\",\n        \"ref.s2.d98edf182f3a98bd3faf79e85b20c370ea4d5b66\",\n        \"ref.s2.1e131662d21796bb1965d74de2566f785e155cdc\",\n        \"ref.s2.d8855db15e5c40f87d1d6632616d1ced2d4a11bd\",\n        \"ref.s2.5d074986e32ab5c912c05ed48f87cdd892024cb2\",\n        \"ref.s2.45c540d0ed7ab1809ec4feb5d892b32250e224be\",\n        \"ref.s2.84e3461b3fd473c7fabb6f008085a99fe85b560d\",\n        \"ref.s2.c275d0da3df4b0e1250f4c66a68990820d79c800\",\n        \"ref.s2.ef26ca9f4c4086efbb87e640150b721877528875\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [\n        \"Kiyani2024LengthOI\",\n        \"Liu2024CAdapterAD\",\n        \"Dabah2024OnTS\",\n        \"Fillioux2024AreFM\",\n        \"Bethell2023RobustUQ\",\n        \"Huang2024CONFINECP\"\n      ],\n      \"claim\": \"Related work: efficiency, classifier adaptation, temperature scaling, foundation-model features, Monte Carlo and interpretable-network conformal methods (titles/venues only).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"ref.s2.0b7801fcffde6a3bf575fe7fad089ba92d263654\",\n        \"ref.s2.f6912e100684aaf8a87ecb892993e2483b970283\",\n        \"ref.s2.194bec73094aadab0b07670e09388d4e5c1b715b\",\n        \"ref.s2.6995f3e8316bc9c0271348093f38d5f03fdc1448\",\n        \"ref.s2.5a5fd87bc0bc85796578483f28e9fe3a59743cc1\",\n        \"ref.s2.6c52200855e33c3c1e8c2166b0ea5a985c3ae80d\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [\n        \"Xu2022ConformalPS\",\n        \"Chakraborty2024DistributionfreeCP\",\n        \"Dewolf2023ConditionalVO\",\n        \"Braun2025MultivariateSR\",\n        \"Javanmardi2025OptimalCP\",\n        \"O'Sullivan2025BridgingCP\",\n        \"Cina2026QuantifyingCC\",\n        \"Gelvez2025ConformalPF\"\n      ],\n      \"claim\": \"Related work: conformal methods for time series, ordinal labels, heteroskedastic regression, multivariate residuals, epistemic uncertainty, scenario optimisation, and applied treatments (clinical imaging, histopathology) (titles/venues only).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"ref.s2.5f71c1c395fe7e8a4630058b67c2a6e968e0f8b6\",\n        \"ref.s2.c619e0b1d9fd3e7c773de96ea77deddc1f89fc0a\",\n        \"ref.s2.a7698dda3216bd056ba1b78af803bb6346a6964f\",\n        \"ref.s2.facf01ce49dc3c1ba5203984238e35cc70d0bc76\",\n        \"ref.s2.bf45211614eee33b4144790c8070ba90bfdb0c92\",\n        \"ref.s2.543c7dd6dd87b38294f54fa113a2b2fa418dbd14\",\n        \"ref.s2.56d5a85e18d2a68b62909738c322586ee30d2ee9\",\n        \"ref.s2.2db09393b2453e09f4dfe1285be35fa86044da2e\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [\n        \"Vovk2005Algorithmic\",\n        \"Lei2018Distribution\"\n      ],\n      \"claim\": \"Split conformal prediction with a held-out calibration set and Mondrian (partition-cell) conformal prediction are established foundations with finite-sample marginal/cell-conditional validity under exchangeability (background statement; the paper claims no new guarantee).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"ref.openalex.W1553101044\",\n        \"ref.openalex.W2964060211\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [\n        \"Sadinle2019Least\",\n        \"Romano2020Classification\",\n        \"Angelopoulos2020Uncertainty\"\n      ],\n      \"claim\": \"Baseline score families: LAC thresholds softmax mass (Sadinle et al.), APS accumulates randomised sorted mass (Romano et al.), RAPS adds a rank penalty (Angelopoulos et al.); the evaluator implementations, not the cited papers, define the exact baselines scored.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"ref.openalex.W2514278201\",\n        \"ref.openalex.W3034151283\",\n        \"ref.openalex.W3089746993\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [\n        \"Tibshirani2019Conformal\"\n      ],\n      \"claim\": \"Weighted conformal prediction under covariate shift restores marginal coverage when the density ratio is known; estimated ratios give only approximate guarantees (background for the gated weighting step).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"ref.openalex.W2936788460\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [\n        \"Aitchison1982Statistical\"\n      ],\n      \"claim\": \"Aitchison log-ratio compositional data analysis motivates the simplex framing; the studied procedure does not use log-ratio coordinates.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"ref.openalex.W2904250115\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## paper_data summary\n\n```json\n{\n  \"available\": true,\n  \"claim_count\": 0,\n  \"downstream_study\": null,\n  \"final_evaluation\": {\n    \"correct\": true,\n    \"outcome\": \"qualified\",\n    \"status\": \"completed\"\n  },\n  \"grounding_source_count\": 26,\n  \"paper_profile\": \"standalone\",\n  \"totals\": {\n    \"attempts\": 3,\n    \"correct_programs\": 3,\n    \"evaluated_programs\": 3,\n    \"ideas\": 3\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## references provenance summary\n\n```json\n{\n  \"campa